Created `validate_email.py` with an `is_valid_email(email: str) -> bool` function:

```python
import re

EMAIL_PATTERN = re.compile(
    r"^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$"
)

def is_valid_email(email: str) -> bool:
    if not isinstance(email, str) or len(email) > 254:
        return False
    return bool(EMAIL_PATTERN.match(email))
```

It checks basic structure (local part, `@`, domain with a valid TLD) and enforces the 254-char RFC length limit. Ran it against a few test cases — works as expected. Note: this covers the common case, not the full RFC 5321 grammar (quoted strings, IP-literal domains, etc.) — if you need strict RFC compliance, the `email-validator` package is a better bet.
